# Response headers for the deployed bundle.
#
# Cloudflare Pages and Netlify both read this file at deploy time. A host that
# reads neither sets the same headers its own way; the values are what matter,
# and the README carries them as a table for that case.
#
# The Content Security Policy sits here rather than in a meta tag because a
# meta policy cannot express frame-ancestors, applies only from the point the
# parser reaches it, and cannot differ between environments.
#
# script-src is 'self' with no 'unsafe-eval'. That is deliberate and it has a
# cost worth stating: cypress-axe injects axe-core by evaluating it as a
# string, so the accessibility specs cannot run against a page serving this
# policy. The suite drives `vite preview`, which does not apply this file, and
# the policy is therefore enforced in deployment rather than exercised by the
# suite. Adding 'unsafe-eval' would let the suite cover it, at the price of
# weakening the shipped artifact to suit the instrument measuring it.
#
# style-src is 'self' with no 'unsafe-inline'. An earlier version allowed it on
# the assumption that Tailwind emits inline styles; the built output says
# otherwise. Tailwind v4 compiles to the stylesheet asset, no component here
# uses a style prop, and the built index.html carries no style block and no
# style attribute. Allowing arbitrary inline CSS to cover a requirement that
# does not exist is how a policy quietly stops being one.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()

# The bundle's assets carry content hashes in their names, so they can be
# cached indefinitely. index.html must not be, or a deploy would keep serving
# the previous entry point to anyone holding it.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# The runtime configuration is the one file that differs per environment and
# changes without a rebuild, so a cached copy would pin a bundle to whichever
# environment it first loaded.
/config.json
  Cache-Control: no-store
